			<div class="content" id="post-content-4815132">
									

<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> A small plastic cup filled with a clear, self-refilling liquid said to be tasteless and have the consistency of toothpaste that, when drunk, takes the last one minute of someone's forgotten memories and replaces them with the last drinker's stored memories. Unless the memory has indications of identity, the drinker will not know whose memory it is but will know that it is not their own. Those who attempt to conjure the stored memory report feeling as though the liquid is in their mouth.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Saliva must enter the liquid for the memory to be stored. The liquid mixes with the saliva on its own, appearing as though nothing was added. When no saliva enters the liquid and another person drinks it, the second drinker is presumed to have permanent retrograde amnesia.</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> After several tests, Level 4 personnel authorized a cross-test between a redacted sentient Euclid and SCP-XXXX. A Level 3 researcher, who was the lead researcher of the Euclid, drank after the anomaly every day, and a Class-D subject would acquire retrograde amnesia before drinking after her, ensuring the anomaly did not gain useful information. The researcher kept a journal of all memories from the transfer, but by the fourth day, she asked to opt-out of the project. By the fifth day, she began showing signs of paranoia, and by the seventh, insanity. After the seventh day, SCP Foundation personnel referred to the researcher as &quot;it&quot;, resulting in violent outbursts. Within a fortnight, it stopped seeing herself as human, claiming to be the redacted SCP but displaying none of its anomalous properties. When informed that the anomaly does not speak, the subject became permanently mute. As her condition degraded, it attempted self-mutilation to &quot;become her true self&quot;; however, personnel stopped it. The subject became increasingly cold and distant. When reintroduced to the anomaly, the two seemed able to communicate, both eventually crying, presumably a consequence of their conversation. When the subject met with its family, the subject showed no signs of recognition. The subject was reclassified as a permanent Class-E and still follows the procedures, now with growing enthusiasm.</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> Everyone has a memory they would be glad they've forgotten, but what if someone could take it? The cup embodies the fear of resurfacing: What might someone do with your memories, and what might someone else's memories do to you?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

