					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cybersqyd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5336470); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5336470&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605818719" alt="cybersqyd" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5336470)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cybersqyd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5336470); return false;" >cybersqyd</a></span> <span class="odate time_1605107433 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">11 Nov 2020 15:10</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4816230">
									

<p>My central concern with this is that your entire 'central narrative' section is backstory: I'm generally of the opinion that a focus on the backstory in a SCP article is bad basically every time it happens and that it's far more interesting to tell a story that hasn't happened yet about the Foundation interacting with a strange thing. Like, in part this is a PoV thing: the story is being told here by Foundation personnel, and so the key story events should happen to Foundation personnel so that we can get first hand accounts of them and the emotional impact they caused. In part, this is also because it often means that the ending of the story is known from the start, which means it can be hard to make it have impact.</p>
<p>Like the fear of being wrongly accused and this anomaly exploring it are both good ideas for a SCP article, its just, this feels like it really needs to explore the effect being wrongly accused has more: the like, deep seated trauma of someone worrying that every time they do something, they'll be accused of a crime; or really just&#8230;more exploration of it than what feels like a token nod to being falsely accused of something sucking.</p>
<p>The nosebleed angle here feels&#8230;weird and tacked on. IMO: it's fine for this to just not do anything if someone drinks from it twice in a row or w/e like, I don't think it needs a weird defence mechanism. The same goes for the euphoria: I think the stuff around memories is interesting enough that you can, and should, let it stand on it's own.</p>

							</div>

								
					
