<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>I decided to have a defence mechanism for people that try to drink it again because I was worried about people switching memories infinitely</p>
</blockquote>
<p>My hot take here is that people switching memories infinitely and the effects it has on them is actually something that could be really interesting to explore here: like, maybe it's two people slowly blending their memories together so much that they end up being almost the same person, with the same shared experiences.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Central Narrative</p>
</blockquote>
<p>My concern with your proposed new Central Narrative is that it feels&#8230;like it's at best tangential to the anomaly? Like, you have this option to really explore what would happen to people if they shared memories and how like, that can affect them as people; and you had the really interesting angle of being falsely accused of something based on a falsified memory; but instead of exploring either, you drag in a GoI and do a generic containment breach story.</p>
<p>Also from a story telling perspective: the story you have here is best told through the point of view of the chaos insurgency operative and like, that's fine but it's not something you can put in a SCP article cos they're obviously not really going to talk about their dastardly schemes in an article, y'know? So it's really worth considering the <em>how</em> when it comes to storytelling: the challenging part of writing a good SCP is that you're very limited in the ways you can tell stories, but this is also what makes it fun to do.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

