			<div class="content" id="post-content-4819724">
									

<p>Hello Doctor Vivaldi! lets take a look :)</p>
<p><strong>SAND</strong></p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><strong>The concept:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Elevator Pitch: A small plastic cup filled with a clear, self-refilling liquid said to be tasteless and have the consistency of toothpaste that, when drunk, takes the last one minute of someone's forgotten memories and replaces them with the last drinker's stored memories. Unless the memory has indications of identity, the drinker will not know whose memory it is but will know that it is not their own. Those who attempt to conjure the stored memory report feeling as though the liquid is in their mouth.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Saliva must enter the liquid for the memory to be stored. The liquid mixes with the saliva on its own, appearing as though nothing was added. When no saliva enters the liquid and another person drinks it, the second drinker is presumed to have permanent retrograde amnesia.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Overall, I don't have much of an issue with the idea. My only real concern here is its purpose, the narrative seems to be completely focused on what happens after its been found. There isn't anything really saying where or how it was found, or what the skips in-universe reason for existing is.</p>
<p><strong>The narrative:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Central Narrative: A previous preeminent lead researcher for this anomaly secretly used it to steal the memories of a recently killed Class-D personnel and reportedly felt significant compassion towards them. The researcher stole SCP-2295 and used it out of the view of others to replace the brains of those who acquired retrograde amnesia from the liquid on the floor. Within a day, researchers looking to improve their efficiency secretly met with the lead researcher, leading to eight percent of all researchers at the site to become amnestic, with some others added to the percentage later. The amnestic researchers were found wandering in the halls. As a result of forgotten passwords, 412 important digital documents were lost. When caught, personnel realized that the offender was actually the Class-D &quot;recently killed&quot;. The Class-D, who had been subject to various tests with SCP-XXXX, gained so much information that he was able to assume his identity. The notoriously curious researcher had forced the Class-D to drink each time after himself in secret and was found amongst Class-D in the true Class-D's cell, evidently confused about his identity. Both the researcher and Class-D were terminated.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The story (to me) is missing a strong setup, I'm unsure on where or how the skip was found. Even the initial tests seem fairly vague, which makes the story feel unfinished and a little hard to follow, as it feels like I've missed context.</p>
<p>Other than that, the story and general progression of events sound fine. My only major issue is the ending, currently feels a bit rushed, as it feels like an excuse to quickly off the characters.- considering you plan to have this be something emotional, I would recommend you show what happens to them after these events, rather than just killing them.</p>
<p><strong>Red or green?</strong><br />
Due to my afore mentioned concerns, no-greenlight.</p>
<p>If you would like me review this (or any concept) further, then please feel free to send me a PM or ask via IRC. Response times generally range from 2-12 hours over PMs. If your looking for additional critiques in general, then I would highly recommend contacting the butterfly squad :)</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Cheers,</p>
<p>-RED</p>

